# Build output.
target/
# Crashing inputs libFuzzer writes when a target fails. Reproducers worth keeping
# get promoted into corpus/ (or into a unit test) by hand.
artifacts/
# Coverage profiles from `cargo fuzz coverage`.
coverage/

# Locally discovered corpus inputs.
#
# A fuzzer writes every interesting input it finds straight into corpus/<target>/, so a
# few minutes of local fuzzing leaves thousands of files there — 9,973 of them while
# these targets were being written. None of that belongs in the repository, and
# `check-added-large-files` will not catch it because the problem is the file count, not
# the file size.
#
# The seeds and regression cases already committed are unaffected: git keeps tracking a
# file it already tracks, ignore rule or not. This only hides *new* files from `git add`.
# To add one deliberately, which is what you want for a crash reproducer, force it:
#
#     git add -f fuzz/corpus/parse_query/regression-some-crash
corpus/
